.TH pageowner 8  "2026-11-26" "USER COMMANDS"
.SH NAME
pageowner \- Show the owners of outstanding page allocations, like page_owner.
Uses Linux eBPF/bcc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pageowner [\-h] [\-v] [\-p PID] [\-T TOP] [\-d] [\-\-max\-allocs MAX_ALLOCS]
.B [\-\-stack\-storage\-size SIZE] [\-\-perf\-max\-stack\-depth DEPTH]
.B [interval] [count]
.SH DESCRIPTION
pageowner records who allocated each block of pages from the page allocator:
the kernel stack trace, the task, the gfp mask, the order and the time of the
allocation. When a block is freed, its record is dropped, so the records show
which pages are still allocated, and by whom.

By default, pageowner summarizes these outstanding pages by kernel stack trace
when Ctrl-C is hit, or every interval seconds, which shows the code paths that
hold the most pages. This is useful to find out where kernel memory that does
not show up in the RSS of any process goes, e.g. for a page leak in a driver,
or memory held in pipes or socket buffers, and which code paths allocate pages
that cause fragmentation. With \-d, pageowner instead prints each outstanding
allocation in the format of /sys/kernel/debug/page_owner, so that the output
can be post-processed by tools written for page_owner, such as page_owner_sort
in tools/mm of the kernel source.

This is like page_owner of the kernel (Documentation/mm/page_owner.rst), but
works on kernels built without CONFIG_PAGE_OWNER or booted without
page_owner=on, as most production kernels are, and needs no reboot. Unlike
page_owner, it only knows about pages allocated while it runs.

When an allocation is split, e.g. by split_page() or when a transparent huge
page is split, and its parts are freed one by one, pageowner keeps tracking
the parts that are still allocated.

This works by tracing the kmem:mm_page_alloc and kmem:mm_page_free
tracepoints.

Since this uses BPF, only the root user can use this tool.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Linux 5.3 or later with CONFIG_BPF, CONFIG_BPF_EVENTS and kernel BTF
(CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF), or BTF for the kernel from BTFHub.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\-h
Print usage message.
.TP
\-v
Print verbose debug output.
.TP
\-p PID
Only track pages allocated by this process (filtered in-kernel). Pages
allocated on its behalf by other tasks, e.g. by kernel threads, are not
tracked.
.TP
\-T TOP
Show only the top TOP stacks, sorted by the number of outstanding pages.
The default is 10. Ignored with \-d.
.TP
\-d
Print each outstanding allocation in the format of
/sys/kernel/debug/page_owner, ordered by PFN, instead of the summary.
.TP
\-\-max\-allocs MAX_ALLOCS
The number of outstanding allocations that can be tracked. The default is
1048576, or the number of pages in the system if that is smaller.
.TP
\-\-stack\-storage\-size SIZE
The number of unique stack traces that can be stored. The default is 10240.
.TP
\-\-perf\-max\-stack\-depth DEPTH
The maximum depth of the stack traces. The default is 127.
.TP
interval
Print a report every interval seconds, instead of only when Ctrl-C is hit.
.TP
count
Number of reports to print before exiting.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Summarize outstanding pages by kernel stack when Ctrl-C is hit:
#
.B pageowner
.TP
Summarize every 5 seconds, 3 times:
#
.B pageowner 5 3
.TP
Only track pages allocated by PID 181:
#
.B pageowner \-p 181
.TP
Show only the top 3 stacks:
#
.B pageowner \-T 3
.TP
Save the outstanding allocations in page_owner format, and sort them by memory with page_owner_sort of the kernel:
#
.B pageowner \-d > page_owner_full.txt
.br
#
.B page_owner_sort \-m page_owner_full.txt sorted_page_owner.txt
.SH FIELDS
The summary shows, for each stack trace:
.TP
pages
The number of outstanding pages allocated from the stack trace, and their
size in KiB.
.TP
allocations
The number of allocations these pages belong to.
.PP
The dump (\-d) shows, for each outstanding allocation:
.TP
order
The order of the block: it consists of 2^order pages. It is lower than the
order of the original allocation, if the allocation was split and some of its
parts were freed.
.TP
mask
The gfp mask of the allocation. The names of the flags are shown on Linux 6.9
and later, which describe the flags in BTF.
.TP
pid, tgid, comm
The thread ID, process ID and command name of the task that allocated the
pages. In interrupt context, this is the interrupted task.
.TP
ts
The time of the allocation, in nanoseconds since boot (CLOCK_MONOTONIC).
.TP
PFN
The page frame number of the first page of the block.
.TP
type
The migrate type of the allocation, derived from the gfp mask.
.SH OVERHEAD
Every page allocation and free is traced: for each allocation, pageowner
records a kernel stack trace and stores the owner in a hash map, and for each
free, it looks the owner up and removes it. The stack trace takes most of the
time, about 85% in the microbenchmark below. The overhead is proportional to
the rate of page allocations, which can be high: hundreds of thousands to
millions per second during heavy page fault or page cache activity. In a
worst-case microbenchmark that did nothing but fault in and free pages on a
single CPU, at about 780000 pages per second, pageowner reduced the
throughput by about 50% (memleak, which also traces page allocations, by about
55%). Frees of pages that are not tracked, e.g. allocated before pageowner
started or filtered out by \-p, cost less: the same microbenchmark slowed down
by about 20%. Use \-p to reduce the overhead to the pages of one process, and a
smaller \-\-perf\-max\-stack\-depth, which truncates the stack traces, to make
them cheaper.

The hash map of the owners is preallocated, and takes about 120 bytes of
kernel memory for each allocation that it can track: about 125 MB for 1048576
allocations, the default unless the system has fewer pages. Use
\-\-max\-allocs to reduce it. The stack traces take about 10 MB by default.

To check the rate of page allocations, count the tracepoint with perf:

#
.B perf stat \-e kmem:mm_page_alloc \-a \-\- sleep 10
.SH CAVEATS
Only pages allocated while pageowner runs are tracked.

Only allocations reported by the kmem:mm_page_alloc tracepoint are tracked.
These do not include pages allocated by alloc_contig_range(), which is used by
e.g. CMA, nor, before Linux 7.4, by the bulk allocator, alloc_pages_bulk(),
which is used by e.g. vmalloc() and some network drivers.

When a page is migrated, e.g. by compaction or NUMA balancing, the new page
is reported as allocated by the migration, while page_owner keeps the
original owner.

The kernel does not run a BPF program if another one is running on the same
CPU, e.g. when a page is freed from an interrupt that arrived while a program
of pageowner was running. Such missed frees, which are more likely under heavy
load, leave pages reported as outstanding until they are allocated again, and
pageowner warns about them.

The stack traces are stored until pageowner exits, even after their pages are
freed. If the storage fills up, new allocations are reported without a stack
trace, and pageowner warns about them.
.SH SOURCE
This is from bcc.
.IP
https://github.com/iovisor/bcc
.PP
Also look in the bcc distribution for a companion _examples.txt file containing
example usage, output, and commentary for this tool.
